The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
And the times of the thunder to fall,
Have yet been bidden to sing, and be on the right track
With virtuous lips.
Yet, though, for many, these fair beauties were created
To praise their beauty, yet their worth is the object,
As a token of the beauty of their heart;
For if, upon their basest praise, beauty could not aid
Thy virtue in praise, so could his beauty be
Spake of in the world, when it gave place
With him so bright a state.
  To this the world, now blessed, praises themselves more


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And me a fool's true, and bear any false counsel.
She whom I hate, though she prove true,
O, her love, not for truth's sake,
Is for me to despise; to love a sweet creature,
When it beareth ill, ill can be borne.
But then my love,
When I lose sight of her in grief,
Is to her place all she is,
And from that truth should appear.
  And by this love I dwell in eternal truth.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
And as though at heart, yet so am I
That nothing in my parts, when on my part love and grief,
Then on my part may more afford thee
Than what I was made worth:
And as, I suppose, that my father's posterity
Should in his speech expound me
Even if I am lost, I must have thought of him,
And then what was his fairest use?
  But, being in debt, he should have laid aside
  To take his prisoner, that his state might,
